{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:53:43Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 145, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Edit", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GSVWE8ddBgQEdMVGBSjA6V", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/home/dev/projects/ai-dev-switchboard/agents/developer.md", "old_string": "## When work comes back to you\n\nReview and test findings come back with a must-fix list. Verify each finding\nis actually correct before implementing it \u2014 if one is wrong, or the fix it\nimplies would be worse than the problem, say so rather than complying\nsilently. Fix the root cause, not the symptom.", "new_string": "## When work comes back to you\n\nReview and test findings come back with a must-fix list. Verify each finding\nis actually correct before implementing it \u2014 if one is wrong, or the fix it\nimplies would be worse than the problem, say so rather than complying\nsilently. Fix the root cause, not the symptom.\n\n**Filter the list by your own scope first.** The build stage dispatches every\nscope of the story concurrently, so if a must-fix item's file falls outside\nthe scope you were given, it is not yours to fix even when you can see\nexactly what the one-line change would be. Leave it untouched, and say in\nyour handoff which item you're leaving and why (naming the file) so the\ndeveloper instance with that scope picks it up. Fixing it yourself risks the\nexact collision \"Your scope\" above warns about \u2014 two concurrent instances\nediting the same file \u2014 even if this run happens to get lucky and the other\ninstance just finds your edit already there."}, "session_id": "7d773e3f-8b00-4053-bc55-6b863de36676"}}
