{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:54:11Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 157, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Write", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Eifc7ywD6WZxDsGiGv3YfP", "input": {"file_path": "/home/dev/projects/ai-dev-switchboard/docs/agent-rework/round-08-logs/retro/report.md", "content": "# Retrospective \u2014 round 8 (Taiga #12: due dates)\n\n## What happened\n\nStory \u2192 design \u2192 build (frontend+backend, parallel) \u2192 review \u2192\nchanges-requested \u2192 build (frontend+backend, parallel) \u2192 review \u2192 approve \u2192\ntest \u2192 pass. One real defect (a `date` field not JSON-serializable in\n`store.py`'s `_save()`) caused the single loop-back; everything else was\nclean. The round finished and merged (`2b6cbd5`).\n\n## What cost time or quality\n\n**A developer instance edited a file outside its declared scope, and got\nlucky rather than caught.** `build/frontend-attempt2.output` fixed\n`backend/app/store.py:47` (`model_dump()` \u2192 `model_dump(mode=\"json\")`) \u2014\nthe exact must-fix item from `review-attempt1.output` \u2014 even though its own\nattempt-1 handoff had explicitly written \"Not in scope: frontend... [backend\nfiles] untouched, another agent's scope\" the other way round. Per\n`app/pipeline.py:88` (`Stage(\"build\", \"developer\", parallel=True, ...)`), the\nfrontend and backend build scopes run **concurrently**, not sequentially \u2014 so\n`build/backend-attempt2` was racing the same file. Its own output\n(`build-backend-attempt2.output`) says: \"the fix was already present in the\nworking tree... likely applied by a prior process/edit before this\ndispatch\" \u2014 i.e. it did not itself collide, but only because it happened to\nread the file after frontend's write landed. `agents/developer.md`'s existing\n\"Your scope\" section already warns that \"two agents editing the same file\nconcurrently is how a run corrupts itself,\" but that warning is framed\naround *reaching for a dependency*, not around *applying a review's must-fix\nitem that happens to sit in another instance's scope* \u2014 which is exactly\nwhat happened here. This round's evidence is that a would-be corruption was\navoided by luck (ordering of two racing writes), not by instruction.\n\n## What I changed\n\n`agents/developer.md`, \"When work comes back to you\" section \u2014 added a\nparagraph directing a developer instance to filter the must-fix list by its\nown declared scope before implementing anything, and to leave out-of-scope\nitems untouched (naming them in the handoff instead) rather than fixing them\nitself, even when the fix is a trivial one-liner it can clearly see.\n\nEvidence: `build-frontend-attempt2.output` (crossed into `store.py`, backend\nscope), `build-backend-attempt2.output` (found the same file already\npatched), `app/pipeline.py:88` (`parallel=True` confirms concurrent\ndispatch, so this was a real race, not a hypothetical one).\n\n## What I deliberately did not change\n\n- **The attempt-1 build-backend \"Edit denied\" blocker.** The developer hit a\n  denied Edit/Bash three times, correctly stopped retrying, and handed off\n  the exact fix content per `agents/developer.md`'s \"When a tool call is\n  denied\" section \u2014 this is that section working as designed, not a gap.\n- **The review round-trip itself** (`changes-requested` \u2192 fix \u2192 `approve`).\n  The review correctly found a real, verified bug (9 failing tests, root\n  cause identified precisely) and the loop-back produced a correct fix. This\n  is the pipeline behaving correctly, not wasted motion.\n- **The `test` stage's \"stale review reattached\" confusion**\n  (`test-attempt1.log:29`, `:39`, `:63`) \u2014 the tester received a dispatch\n  whose trailing section quoted `review-attempt1`'s `changes-requested` text\n  (9 failures) even though the round had already moved on to\n  `review-attempt2`'s `approve`. The tester correctly distrusted the stale\n  text, verified independently (ran the suite itself, read the file), and\n  reached the right verdict (`pass`) without wasting significant effort. Its\n  own instructions (\"Evidence\" section of `agents/tester.md`) already produce\n  this behavior, so there's nothing to fix on the agent-instruction side.\n  This looks like a **dispatch-assembly quirk in the machinery** (which prior\n  review gets concatenated into the test-stage prompt) \u2014 flagged below, not\n  fixed here, since it's outside `agents/*.md` and did not actually cause a\n  wrong outcome this round.\n\n## Needs a human\n\n- Check `app/pipeline_runner.py` / `app/pipeline.py`'s prompt assembly for\n  the `test` stage: it appears to attach the review text from the attempt\n  that *triggered* the current build/review loop (i.e. the earlier\n  `changes-requested` review), rather than only the latest (`approve`)\n  review, when building the test-stage dispatch. It didn't cause a wrong\n  verdict this round because the tester independently re-verified, but a\n  less skeptical prompt or a genuinely ambiguous case could be misled by\n  which review is authoritative. Machinery, not `agents/*.md` \u2014 out of this\n  role's remit.\n\nVERDICT: done\n"}, "session_id": "7d773e3f-8b00-4053-bc55-6b863de36676"}}
